REGIONAL REGULATION OF SUMEDANG REGENCY

NUMBER 5 OF 2024

CONCERNING

WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF SUMEDANG,

Considering:

a. that every person has the right to be free from discriminatory treatment and to obtain equal opportunities and benefits in various fields of development in Sumedang Regency;
b. children are one of the implementations of concurrent government affairs policies that are the authority of regional government, which constitute an integral dimension unity starting from planning, budgeting, implementation, monitoring, evaluation, supervision and reporting on policies, programs, and regional development activities;
c. that to realize legal certainty and provide legal protection guarantees as well as synergy and integration in the implementation of policies, programs, and activities of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Sumedang Regency Region, it is necessary to regulate and stipulate by Regional Regulation;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to stipulate a Regional Regulation concerning Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1950) as amended by Law Number 4 of 1968 concerning the Establishment of Purwakarta Regency and Subang Regency by amending Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1968 Number 31, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2851);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 237, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5946);
6. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
7. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
8. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4270);
9. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
10. Law Number 10 of 2012 concerning Ratification of the Optional Protocol to The Convention on The Rights of The Child on The Sale of Children, Child Prostitution and Child Pornography (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 149, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5330);
11. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
12. Law Number 14 of 2019 concerning Social Workers (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2019 Number 182, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6397);
13. Law Number 12 of 2022 concerning the Crime of Sexual Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 120, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6792);
14. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation of Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
15. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 4818);
16. Government Regulation Number 59 of 2019 concerning Implementation of Child Protection Coordination (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2019 Number 160, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6382);
17. Government Regulation Number 78 of 2021 concerning Special Protection for Children (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2021 Number 174, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6704);
18. Presidential Regulation Number 18 of 2014 concerning Protection and Empowerment of Women and Children in Social Conflict (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 44);
19. Presidential Regulation Number 75 of 2020 concerning Implementation of the Rights of Child Victims and Child Witnesses (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2020 Number 164);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SUMEDANG REGENCY

and

REGENT OF SUMEDANG

HAS DECIDED:

To Stipulate:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION.

Part Two Principles

Article 2

The Implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection is carried out based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;
b. justice:c. substantive equality;
d. Participatory:e. non-discrimination;
f. best interests of the Child;
g. good governance; and
h. sustainability.

Article 4

This Regional Regulation aims to:
a. realize Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Regency Area that is effective, efficient, optimal, transparent, and accountable, as well as administratively orderly;
b. realize Gender Responsive development by integrating Gender Perspective into planning, budgeting, implementation, monitoring, evaluation, supervision, and reporting on policies, programs, activities and sub-activities of development in the Regency Area;
c. realize Gender Equality in family, community, national and state life;
d. realize Gender Responsive Regional budget management;
e. support efforts to increase the capacity of forums, institutions and/or organizations for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;
f. increase the synergy of Regency Regional Government and the community in the development of Gender Responsive Women's Empowerment and Child Protection; and
g. increase Community Participation in the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Regency Area.

Article 5

The scope of this Regional Regulation regulates:
a. Quality of Life of Women;
b. Protection of Women;
c. Protection of Children;
d. Improvement of Family Quality;
e. Gender and Child Data System;
f. cooperation;
g. Community Participation;
h. coordination;
i. monitoring;
j. reporting;
k. awards;
l. funding; and
m. sanctions.

Paragraph 1 General

Article 7

The implementation of Quality of Life of Women in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 6 paragraph (1), consists of:
a. implementation of PUG in the Regency Area;
b. Institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus;
c. Women's Empowerment in the political, legal, social, and economic fields in Mass Organizations in the Regency Area;
d. strengthening and development of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area; and
e. Capacity Building of Women in the Regency Area.

Article 9

1. Institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 7 letter b, is implemented through:
a. fulfillment of PUG Institutionalization standards;
b. integration of Gender Perspective;
c. preparation of planning and budgeting; and
d. advocacy, facilitation, and provision of technical assistance.
2. Integration of Gender Perspective as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, is incorporated into the policies and development programs of the Regency Area.
3. Policies and development programs of the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (2), consist of:
a. RPJPD;
b. RPJMD;
c. RKPD;
d. Renstra; and
e. Renja.
4. Policies and development programs of the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (2), include:
a. planning;
b. preparation;
c. implementation;
d. monitoring;
e. control; and
f. evaluation.
5. Preparation of planning and budgeting as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, by considering equal Access, Participation, control, and Benefits between women and men, which takes into account the aspirations, needs, and specific problems of women and men.
6. Advocacy, facilitation and provision of technical assistance as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, is directed to institutions that have legislative functions and vertical agencies in the Regency Area related to the implementation of PUG.

Article 10

1. Institutionalization of PUG within the Regency Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 7 letter b, is implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
2. PUG Institutionalization standards as referred to in paragraph (1), consist of:
a. commitment of the Regent;
b. policies and programs;
c. institutions;
d. resources;
e. Sex-Disaggregated Data and Gender statistics;
f. implementation methods; and
g. Community Participation.
3. PUG Institutionalization procedures as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. coordination and synchronization of policy formulation for PUG implementation in the Regency Area;
b. coordination and synchronization of PUG implementation in the Regency Area;
c. policy advocacy and assistance for PUG implementation in the Regency Area including PPRG under Regency Area authority; and
d. socialization of policies for PUG implementation in the Regency Area including PPRG under Regency Area authority.
4. Coordination and synchronization of policy formulation for PUG implementation in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (3) letter a, includes:
a. harmonization and synchronization of laws and regulations, Regency Area legal products, and operational policies regarding PUG; and
b. preparation of RPJPD, RPJMD, RKPD, Renstra, and Renja as well as Gender Responsive Regency Regional Apparatus budgets.
5. Coordination and synchronization of PUG implementation in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (3) letter b, includes:
a. establishment of PUG Working Group;
b. preparation of annual and 5 (five) year PUG Working Group action plans;
c. PUG Working Group coordination meetings; and
d. integration of Sex-Disaggregated Data into the Gender and Child Data System.
6. Policy advocacy and assistance for PUG implementation in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (3) letter c, includes:
a. technical coordination meetings for PUG implementation;
b. technical guidance and assistance for PPRG in the preparation of Gender Responsive programs, activities, and budgets; and
c. facilitation in the preparation of Gender Responsive Regency Area policies.
7. Socialization of policies for PUG implementation in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (3) letter d, includes:
a. dissemination of policies for PUG and PPRG implementation to stakeholders; and
b. strengthening of Community Participation in PUG implementation.

Article 14

1. Women's Empowerment in the political, legal, social, and economic fields in Community Organizations in the Regency as referred to in Article 7 letter c, shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
2. The standards for Women's Empowerment in the political, legal, social, and economic fields in Community Organizations in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. institutional;
b. resources;
c. services and programs; and
d. monitoring, evaluation, and reporting.
3. The procedures for Women's Empowerment in the political, legal, social, and economic fields in Community Organizations in the Regency, as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. socialization of increasing women's Participation in the political, legal, social, and economic fields in the Regency; and
b. policy advocacy and assistance for increasing women's Participation in politics, law, social, and economic matters in the Regency.
4. Socialization of increasing women's Participation in the political, legal, social, and economic fields in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (3) letter a, includes:
a. the role of Community Organizations in Women's Empowerment;
b. the role of women in politics;
c. women's leadership;
d. legal literacy for women;
e. social literacy for women; and
f. women's entrepreneurship.
5. Advocacy … (5) Policy advocacy and assistance for increasing women's Participation in politics, law, social, and economic matters in the Regency, as referred to in paragraph (3) letter b, includes:
a. strengthening policies on increasing the Participation of Community Organizations, as well as professional institutions and the Business World for Women's Empowerment;
b. developing synergistic cooperation models between the government and Community Organizations as well as professional institutions and the Business World for solving Women's Empowerment problems;
c. developing communication forums for Community Organizations as well as professional institutions and the Business World for Women's Empowerment;
d. creating a database of Community Organizations and professional institutions as well as the Business World for Women's Empowerment; and
e. conducting studies on the Participation of Community Organizations as well as professional institutions and the Business World for Women's Empowerment.

Article 17

Strengthening and Development of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions in the Regency as referred to in Article 7 letter d, shall be implemented through:
a. increasing the capacity of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions;
b. increasing …b. increasing the capacity of human resources managing Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions;
c. assisting the implementation of Women's Empowerment services;
d. strengthening networks among Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions in the Regency;
e. facilitating efforts to meet the standards of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions; and
f. developing communication, information, and education about Women's Empowerment services.

Part Two Fulfillment of Women's Rights

Article 22

1. Fulfillment of the rights of every Woman in the Regency Area, includes:
a. the right to life;
b. the right to form a family and continue offspring;
c. the right to self-development;
d. the right to obtain justice;
e. the right to personal freedom;
f. the right to security;
g. the right to welfare; and
h. the right to participate in government.
2. The right to life as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a, includes:
a. the right to sustain life and improve one's standard of living;
b. the right to obtain tranquility, security, peace, happiness, physical and spiritual welfare; and
c. the right to a good and healthy environment.
3. The right to form a family and continue offspring as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, namely the right to form a Family and continue offspring through lawful marriage in accordance with religion and the provisions of laws and regulations governing marriage.
4. The right to self-development as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, includes:
a. the right to fulfillment of basic needs to grow and develop properly;
b. the right to protection for personal development, to obtain education, educate oneself and improve quality of life to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
c. the right to develop and obtain Benefits from science and technology, arts and culture in accordance with human dignity for the welfare of oneself, the nation, and humanity;
d. the right to communicate and obtain information necessary to develop oneself and one's social environment in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
e. the right to seek, obtain, possess, store, process, and convey information using all types of available means in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
f. the right to fight for the right to self-development, both individually and collectively, to build society, nation, and state in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
g. the right to perform social work in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
h. the right to participate in the planning and implementation of government policies in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
i. the right to organize and form organizations in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations; and
j. the right to organize education and teaching in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms.
5. The right to obtain justice as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, includes:
a. the right to receive equal treatment without Discrimination;
b. the right to obtain justice;
c. the right to submit applications, complaints, and lawsuits, whether in criminal, civil, or administrative cases; and
d. the right to be tried through a free and impartial judicial process, in accordance with procedural law that guarantees objective examination by honest and fair judges to obtain a just and correct decision.
6. The right to personal freedom as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e, includes:
a. the right not to be enslaved or subjugated, trafficked, and all acts with similar purposes;
b. the right to personal integrity, both spiritual and physical;
c. the right not to be the object of research without one's consent; and
d. the right to freely express and convey opinions with due regard to religious values, morality, public order, public interest, and national integrity.
7. The right to security as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, includes:
a. the right to protection of oneself, Family, honor, dignity, and property rights;
b. the right to recognition before the law as a human person; and
c. the right to be free from threats to do or not do something that violates religious, social, and legal norms.
8. The right to welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) letter g, includes:
a. the right to have welfare, either alone or together; and
b. the right to perform work and receive fair wages in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
9. The right to participate in government as referred to in paragraph (1) letter h, includes:
a. the right to be elected and to vote in general elections;
b. the right to pursue a career in government; and
c. the right to convey aspirations directly or through intermediaries in government.

Article 24

1. The Regency Regional Government undertakes prevention efforts as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (3) letter a, to prevent Women from the impacts of Conflict situations and events.
2. The Regency Regional Government in undertaking prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by:
a. providing data and studies on women in Conflict;
b. increasing …b. increasing public awareness, customary institutions or customary and social institutions, interfaith communication forums to provide protection for women in Conflict;
c. increasing the role of mass media in providing dissemination and information on protection of women in Conflict;
d. increasing the role of women's service units to provide protection for women in Conflict;
e. conducting training in the prevention of Violence against women in Conflict; and
f. facilitating the addition of provision of public spaces/urban green open spaces for women.

Article 25

1. The Regency Regional Government provides services to women as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (3) letter b.
2. The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (1), is provided to:a. women victims as a result of the occurrence of Conflict; and b. women victims of Violence.
3. The provision of services to women as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. providing Special Protection;
b. providing services to women victims of Violence;
c. providing services for the fulfillment of specific basic needs for women victims as a result of the occurrence of Conflict; and
d. improvement of facilities needed by women in Conflict.
4. In the provision of services as referred to in paragraph (1) the Regency Regional Government must provide data on women victims of Conflict in the Region.
5. Special Protection as referred to in paragraph (3) letter a includes efforts to rescue and protect:
a. women from experiencing Violence; and
b. defenders of women's human rights.
6. The provision of services to women victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (3) letter b includes services for:
a. complaints;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. reproductive health;
d. Social Rehabilitation;
e. Social Reintegration; and
f. legal assistance and/or accompaniment.
7. Improvement of facilities needed by women in Conflict as referred to in paragraph (3) letter d includes:
a. improvement of health service facilities;
b. improvement of reproductive health service facilities;
c. provision of decent, safe, and Gender Responsive housing and homes;
d. facilitation in the improvement of new housing construction, public facilities and infrastructure; and
e. provision of clean water and sanitation for women.

Article 28

1. Every woman in the Regency Area as a witness and victim as referred to in Article 27 letter a, has the right to:
a. obtain protection for the security of herself, Family, and property, and be free from threats related to testimony that will be, is being, or has been given;
b. participate in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;
c. provide testimony without pressure;
d. obtain an interpreter;
e. be free from ensnaring questions;
f. obtain information regarding case developments;
g. obtain information regarding court decisions;
h. obtain information in the event the convict is released;
i. have her identity kept confidential;
j. obtain a new identity;
k. obtain temporary residence;
l. obtain a new residence;
m. obtain reimbursement of transportation costs according to needs;
n. obtain legal advice;
o. obtain temporary living cost assistance up to the time limit of protection ends; and/or
p. obtain assistance.
2. Women in the Regency Area as victims of gross human rights violations, victims of terrorism crimes, victims of human trafficking crimes, victims of torture crimes, victims of sexual violence crimes, and victims of severe persecution, in addition to being entitled to the rights as referred to in paragraph (1), are also entitled to obtain:
a. medical assistance;
b. psychosocial and psychological rehabilitation assistance;
c. compensation; and
d. restitution.
3. Restitution as referred to in paragraph (2) letter d, is in the form of:
a. compensation for loss of wealth or income;
b. compensation arising from suffering directly related as a result of the crime; and/or
c. reimbursement of medical and/or psychological treatment costs.
4. The granting of rights to women as witnesses and victims as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations governing the protection of witnesses and victims.

Article 29

1. Every Woman in the Regency Area as a Victim of domestic violence as referred to in Article 27 letter b, has the right to obtain:
a. protection from the Family, police, prosecutor's office, court, advocates, social institutions, or other parties either temporarily or based on a protection order from the court;
b. health services according to medical needs;
c. special handling related to victim confidentiality;
d. assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations; and
e. spiritual guidance services.
2. The granting of rights to women in the Regency Area as Victims of domestic violence as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations governing the elimination of domestic Violence.

Article 31

1. The Regent coordinates the implementation of Protection of Women from GBV in disasters.
2. Coordination of the implementation of Protection of Women from GBV in disasters as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by:
a. ensuring the existence of programs and activities carried out by Regency Regional Apparatus regarding the protection of women's rights from GBV in disasters;
b. ensuring the fulfillment of specific needs of women including persons with disabilities and the elderly;
c. monitoring the development of services related to victim handling;
d. building a mechanism for implementing monitoring, evaluation, and reporting of Protection of Women from GBV services in disasters periodically and in tiers; and
e. preparing an action plan for Protection of Women from GBV in disasters.

Article 32

The Regent coordinates the Protection of Women from GBV in disasters in the Regency Area, as referred to in Article 31 paragraph (1), carried out by:
a. providing information and Disaggregated Data based on gender, age, and type of GBV vulnerability;
b. building stakeholder commitment to prevention, handling, and Protection of Women from GBV in disasters;
c. providing women-friendly space facilities, as well as Gender Responsive facilities and infrastructure in emergency response and rehabilitation conditions that accommodate local resources;
d. providing complaint, handling, and referral services needed by women victims of Violence that accommodate local wisdom; and
e. ensuring victims or survivors obtain empowerment services according to their potential, abilities, and needs so they can recover quickly, become independent, and productive.

Part Six Protection of Women with Disabilities

Article 34

1. Protection of women with disabilities is carried out through the fulfillment of women's rights as persons with disabilities.
2. The rights of women as persons with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (1), include:
a. life;
b. freedom from stigma;
c. privacy;
d. justice and legal protection;
e. education;
f. employment, entrepreneurship, and cooperatives;
g. health;
h. politics;
i. religion;
j. sports;
k. culture and tourism;
l. social welfare;
m. accessibility;
n. public services;
o. protection from disasters;
p. habilitation and rehabilitation;
q. Concessions;
r. data collection;
s. living independently and being involved in society;
t. expressing, communicating, and obtaining information;
u. moving places and citizenship; and
v. freedom from acts of Discrimination, neglect, torture, and Exploitation.
3. In addition to having the rights of Women as persons with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (2), women with disabilities have the right:
a. to reproductive health;
b. to accept or reject the use of contraceptives;
c. to obtain greater protection from multiple Discrimination; and
d. to obtain greater protection from acts of Violence, including sexual Violence and Exploitation.

Article 40

1. Achievement of KTP prevention involving parties in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 37 letter a shall be measured by criteria.
2. Criteria for achievement of KTP prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) shall consist of:
a. compliance with implementation of policies regarding KTP prevention;
b. impact of implementation of programs, activities, and budget regarding KTP prevention;
c. impact of communication, information, and education activities regarding KTP prevention;
d. synergy of programs related to KTP prevention;
e. integration of KTP prevention into the Regency regional development planning; and f. Community Participation and cooperation with the Business Sector and mass media in prevention

Article 42

Provision of services for women victims of Violence requiring coordination in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 37 letter b, shall be implemented through:
a. receiving reports and/or outreach to Victims of Violence;
b. providing information on the rights of Victims of Violence;
c. facilitating provision of health services;
d. facilitating provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitating provision of psychosocial services, Social Rehabilitation, social empowerment, Repatriation, and Social Reintegration;
f. providing legal services;
g. identifying economic empowerment needs;
h. identifying temporary shelter needs for Victims of Violence and Families of Victims of Violence that need to be fulfilled immediately;
i. facilitating needs of Victims of Violence with disabilities;
j. coordinating and cooperating on fulfillment of rights of Victims of Violence with other institutions; and
k. monitoring fulfillment of rights of victims of Violence by law enforcement apparatus during judicial proceedings.

Article 43

1. Provision of services for women victims of Violence requiring coordination in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 37 letter b shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
2. Standards for provision of services for women victims of Violence requiring coordination in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1) shall include:
a. receiving reports and/or outreach to victims of Violence;
b. providing information on the rights of victims of Violence;
c. facilitating provision of health services;
d. facilitating provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitating provision of psychosocial services, Social Rehabilitation, social empowerment, Repatriation, and Social Reintegration;
f. providing legal services;
g. identifying economic empowerment needs;
h. identifying temporary shelter needs for victims of Violence and Families of victims of Violence that need to be fulfilled immediately;
i. facilitating needs of Victims of Violence with disabilities;
j. coordinating and cooperating on fulfillment of rights of victims of Violence with other institutions; and
k. monitoring fulfillment of rights of victims of Violence by law enforcement apparatus during judicial proceedings.
3. Procedures for provision of services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall consist of:
a. provision of Community complaint services for women victims of Violence in the Regency Area, covering:
1\. establishment of UPTD PPA in the Regency Area;
2\. implementation of UPTD PPA services; and
3\. provision and strengthening of human resources for service providers through training and certification of Women's rights protection services.
b. coordination and synchronization of implementation of provision of further referral services for women victims of violence in the Regency Area, covering:
1\. cross-Regency Regional Apparatus coordination for implementation of provision of KTP case services;
2\. development of policies and implementation of integrated KTP case handling systems across related Regency Regional Apparatus; and
3\. data collection, monitoring, and evaluation of KTP case handling.

Article 46

Strengthening and development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 37 letter c shall be implemented through:
a. capacity building of human resources of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions for victims of Violence in the Regency Area;
b. provision of specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions in the Regency Area;
c. development of communication, information, and education strategies on protection of women's rights in the Regency Area;
d. strengthening networks among Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area;
e. facilitating efforts to fulfill standards of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area; and
f. development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area.

Article 47

1. Strengthening and development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 37 letter c shall be implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
2. The standards for strengthening and development as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. measured and certified human resource capacity of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions for victims of Violence in the Regency Area;
b. specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions in the Regency Area that are on target and appropriate;
c. effective and efficient communication, information, and education strategies regarding the protection of women's rights;
d. networks among Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area;
e. Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area have agreed service standards and operational system procedures; and
f. Measured development of Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area.
3. The procedures for strengthening and development as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. policy advocacy and assistance in providing service facilities and infrastructure for women victims of Violence in the Regency Area;
b. capacity building of human resources of service provider institutions for handling women victims of Violence in the Regency Area;
c. provision of specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions in the Regency Area; and
d. strengthening networks among Women's Protection Service Provider Institutions in the Regency Area.

Part Three Fulfillment of Children's Rights

Paragraph 1 General

Article 52

1. The Regency Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility in the implementation of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights.
2. The Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. the right to live, grow, develop, and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and dignity, and receive protection from violence and Discrimination;
b. the right to a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
c. the right to worship according to their religion, think, and express themselves in accordance with their level of intelligence and age under the guidance of Parents or Guardians;
d. the right to know their Parents, be raised, and be cared for by their own Parents;
e. the right to be cared for or adopted as a foster Child or adopted Child by others in the event that for any reason the Parents cannot guarantee the growth and development of the Child, or the Child is in a state of neglect;
f. the right to obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual, and social needs;
g. the right to obtain education and teaching in the context of developing their personality and level of intelligence in accordance with their interests and talents;
h. the right to receive protection in educational units from sexual crimes and Violence committed by educators, education personnel, fellow students, and/or other parties;
i. the right to express and be heard their opinions, receive, seek, and provide information in accordance with their level of intelligence and age for their self-development in accordance with the values of decency and propriety;
j. the right to rest and utilize leisure time, socialize with children of the same age, play, recreate, and create in accordance with their interests, talents, and level of intelligence for self-development;
k. the right to receive protection from Discrimination, Exploitation, both economic and sexual, neglect, cruelty, violence, and abuse, injustice, and other mistreatment while in the care of Parents, Guardians, or any other party responsible for care;
1. the right to be cared for by their own Parents, unless there are valid reasons and/or legal rules showing that the separation and/or divorce is in the best interests of the Child and is a last consideration;
m. the right to meet directly and have regular personal contact with both Parents, receive care, maintenance, education and protection for the growth and development process from both Parents in accordance with their abilities, talents, and interests, obtain living expenses from both Parents, and obtain other Children's Rights, in the event of separation and/or divorce as referred to in letter 1;
n. the right to obtain protection from abuse in political activities, involvement in armed conflicts, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of Violence, involvement in warfare, and sexual crimes;
o. the right to obtain protection from being the target of persecution, torture, or imposition of inhuman punishment, and to obtain freedom in accordance with the law;
p. the right to receive humane treatment and placement separated from adults, obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of applicable legal efforts, and defend themselves and obtain justice before an objective and impartial juvenile court in a closed session to the public, in the case of a Child deprived of liberty;
q. the right to confidentiality in the case of a Child who becomes a victim or perpetrator of sexual Violence or who is in conflict with the law;
r. the right to receive legal assistance and other assistance in the event that a child becomes a victim or perpetrator of a criminal act; and
s. the right to receive protection and maintenance after divorce.
2. The implementation of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), is carried out in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations governing the protection of Children.

Article 54

The institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government, non-government, and Business World institutions in the Regency as referred to in Article 53 letter a is implemented through:
a. implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in efforts to prevent violations of children's rights in government, non-government, media, and Business World institutions in the Regency;
b. increasing participation and the role of Children's Forums or the like in the Regency;
c. strengthening the institutions and resources of government, non-government, media, and Business World institutions in the Regency;
d. strengthening networks among government, non-government, media, and Business World institutions in the Regency; and
e. strengthening cross-institutional cooperation among government, non-government, media, and Business World to realize Child-Friendly Regency, Child-Friendly Sub-district, and Child-Friendly Village/Sub-district.

Article 55

1. The institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government, non-government, and Business World institutions in the Regency as referred to in Article 53 letter a is implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
2. The standards for institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government, non-government, and Business World institutions in the Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
3. The procedures for institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. policy advocacy and assistance for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government, non-government, media, and Business World institutions in the Regency, including:
1\. harmonization and synchronization of laws and regulations and legal products of the Regency related to children's rights and efforts to Fulfill Children's Rights;
2\. preparation of RPJPD, RPJMD, RKPD, Renstra, and Renja documents with a child rights perspective;
3\. technical coordination meetings for the implementation of policies, strategies and programs, as well as monitoring and evaluation of efforts to Fulfill Children's Rights;
4\. technical guidance and assistance in the preparation of programs, activities, and budgets that are responsive to Children's Rights and their implementation;
5\. facilitation in the preparation of Regency policies that are responsive to Children's Rights and their implementation;
6\. dissemination of policies on the Fulfillment of Children's Rights to stakeholders; and
7\. strengthening Community Participation in efforts to Fulfill Children's Rights; and
b. coordination and synchronization of the Institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the Regency, including:
1\. establishment of working groups, focal points, technical teams, and task forces for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights;
2\. preparation of work plans for working groups, focal points, technical teams, and task forces for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights;
3\. coordination meetings to strengthen working groups, focal points, technical teams, and task forces for the Fulfillment of Children's Rights; and
4\. integration of data on the situation and condition of Children by gender in the Fulfillment of Children's Rights into a data system that can be accessed through official publication media.

Article 56

1. The achievement of institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights in government institutions in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 53 letter a is measured by criteria.
2. The criteria for achieving institutionalization of the Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1) consist of:
a. effectiveness of policies that are responsive to Children's Rights;
b. value and ranking of the Regency Area in the cluster of Fulfillment of Children's Rights that receives the predicate of Child-Friendly;
c. index of fulfillment of Children's Rights;
d. Child Protection Index;
e. number of sub-districts with the predicate of Child-Friendly sub-district in the cluster of Fulfillment of Children's Rights; and
f. number of Villages and sub-districts with the ranking of Child-Friendly Village/sub-district in the cluster of Fulfillment of Children's Rights.

Article 57

Strengthening and development of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 53 letter b is implemented through:
a. capacity building of resources of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area;
b. assistance in the implementation of services for improving the quality of life of Children in the Regency Area;
c. development of materials and implementation of communication, information, and education on the Fulfillment of Children's Rights for Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area;
d. strengthening networks among Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area;
e. facilitation of the implementation of standardization of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area; and
f. development of Service Provider Institutions for Improving the Quality of Life of Children in the Regency Area.

Article 62

1. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 61, is provided to:
a. Children in emergency situations;
b. Children in conflict with the law;
c. Children from minority and isolated groups;
d. Children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. Children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. Children who are victims of pornography;
g. Children with HIV and AIDS;
h. Children victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. Children victims of bullying;
j. Children victims of sexual crimes;
k. Children victims of terrorism networks;
l. Children with disabilities;
m. Children victims of abuse and neglect;
n. Children with deviant social behavior; and
o. Children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
2. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out through efforts to:
a. rapid handling, including treatment and/or rehabilitation physically, psychologically, and socially, as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;
b. psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery;
c. provision of social assistance for Children from underprivileged Families; and
d. provision of protection and assistance in every judicial process.
3. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1) is provided at UPTD/UPTD PPA and Puspaga.
4. Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented quickly, comprehensively, and in an integrated manner.

Article 63

In addition to being obligated and responsible for implementing Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 61 and Article 62, the Regency Regional Government is obligated to organize:
a. protection of Children in Conflict;
b. protection of Children as Witnesses and/or Victims;
c. Protection of Children from acts of KGB in disasters; and
d. protection of Children with disabilities.

Article 68

In addition to obtaining rights as persons with disabilities as referred to in Article 34 paragraph (2) and Article 67, Children with disabilities have the right to:
a. obtain Special Protection from Discrimination, neglect, harassment, Exploitation, as well as violence and sexual crimes;
b. obtain care and upbringing by Family or substitute Family to grow and develop optimally;
c. have their interests protected in decision-making;
d. treatment of Children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and Rights of Children;
e. fulfillment of special needs;
f. equal treatment with other Children to achieve social integration and individual development; and
g. obtain social assistance.

Article 69

The Regency Regional Government in implementing the organization of Special Protection of Children in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 61, provides:
a. social workers and social welfare personnel;
b. competent and trained health personnel;
c. spiritual/worship guidance officers;
d. educators and education personnel; and/or e. legal aid personnel.

Article 71

Prevention of KTA involving parties in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 70 letter a shall be implemented through:
a. implementation of policies, programs, and activities for KTA prevention involving parties in the Regency Area;
b. provision of education on KTA prevention activities involving parties in the Regency Area;
c. enhancement of the participation of the Community, Business World, media, and Children in KTA prevention in the Regency Area; and
d. strengthening of cooperation to realize KLA, child-friendly sub-districts, and child-friendly Villages/sub-districts.

Article 74

Provision of services for AMPK requiring coordination in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 70 letter b shall be implemented through:
a. receiving reports or outreach to AMPK;
b. providing information about the rights of AMPK;
c. facilitating the provision of health services;
d. facilitating the provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitating the provision of psychosocial services, Social Rehabilitation, social empowerment, and Social Reintegration;
f. provision of legal services;
g. identifying temporary shelter needs;
h. facilitating the needs of Victims with disabilities; and
i. cooperating on the fulfillment of AMPK rights with other institutions.

Article 77

Strengthening and development of Service Provider Institutions for AMPK in the Regency Area as referred to in Article 70 letter c shall be implemented through:
a. capacity building of institutional resources of service provider institutions for handling AMPK in the Regency Area;
b. provision of assistance for special needs for AMPK in the Regency Area;
c. provision of communication, information, and education on AMPK protection in the Regency Area;
d. strengthening of networks among service provider institutions for protection of AMPK in the Regency Area;
e. facilitation of the implementation of standardization of service provider institutions for protection of AMPK in the Regency Area; and
f. development of service provider institutions for protection of AMPK in the Regency Area.

Article 87

Strengthening and development of Service Provider Institutions for Improving Family Quality in realizing Gender Equality and Children's Rights in the Regency Area, is implemented through:
a. capacity building of human resources of Service Provider Institutions for Improving Family Quality in the Regency Area;
b. strengthening networks of Service Provider Institutions for Improving Family Quality in the Regency Area;
c. facilitation of standardization implementation of Service Provider Institutions for Improving Family Quality in the Regency Area; and
d. development of Service Provider Institutions for Improving Family Quality in the Regency Area.

Article 92

1. Provision of services for Families in realizing Gender Equality and Children's Rights in the Regency Area, is implemented in accordance with standards and procedures.
2. Standards for provision of services for Families as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. institutional;
b. human resources;
c. facilities and infrastructure;
d. availability of communication, information, education materials, and modules;
e. service delivery including prevention of child marriage; and
f. synergy and coordination.
3. Procedures for provision of services for Families as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by implementing comprehensive service provision for Families in realizing Gender Equality and Children's Rights in the Regency Area.
4. Further provisions regarding service delivery and prevention of child marriage are further regulated by Regent Regulation.
